In addition to supporting MOFs crystals, suitable substrates for air pollution control should also be commercially available, cost-effective and eco-friendly (Martins & da Graça, 2018). Although most commercial filters are currently fabricated from petroleum-based materials, cellulose-based substrates enjoy popularity for their biodegradability, renewability, natural abundance and good processability (Da Silva Pinto, Sierra-Avila, & Hinestroza, 2012; Duan et al., 2018; Ma, Zhang, Nie, Tun, Song et al., 2019; Ma, Zhang, Nie, Tun, Yang et al., 2019; Mirkovic, Lei, Ljubic, & Zhu, 2019; Sui et al., 2018). A very important branch of cellulose-based materials is paper (He, Cho, & Won, 2016). Paper has favorable properties in addition to the merits of cellulose,
including recyclability and mechanical flexibility (Barhoum et al., 2014). Moreover, paper materials are low-cost, widely available and highly industrialized, and have been used in a wide variety of forms such as filter core in fresh air systems (Niu, Ma, Hu, & Zhou, 2007; Xiong, Yang, Zhu, Chen, & Dong, 2017). Collectively, these advantages make it meaningful to employ paper as the substrate of MOFs.
However, the exploration of MOFs-paper materials is still at early stage, although studies on the integration of MOFs and cellulose fibers have emerged in recent years. The integration of MOFs and cellulose fibers can be realized by virtue of complexation between the metal ions in MOFs and the carboxyl groups on chemically modified fibers (Da Silva Pinto et al., 2012; Duan et al., 2018), or by electrostatic interaction and hydrogen bonds between MOFs and fibers (Ma, Zhang, Nie, Tan, Song et al., 2019), or with surface modification agents such as cellulose nanofiber (CNF) (Ma, Zhang, Nie, Tan, Song et al., 2019; Ma, Zhang, Nie, Tan, Yang et al., 2019) and dopamine (DA) (Mirkovic et al., 2019). These strategies, nevertheless, do not guarantee the successful fabrication of MOFs-paper materials. On one hand, it is ineffective to transform the MOFs-loaded fibers into paper, since the fibers would suffer from a heavy loss of MOFs crystals during the vigorous paper-making procedures. On the other hand, it is also challenging to effectively load MOFs crystals on paper, since the fibers in paper are interwoven and closely packed (Mirkovic et al., 2019; Shen, Song, Qian, & Yang, 2010; Yang, Zhang, Song, & Yang, 2017).
To integrate crystals component into paper, in paper-making industry, the common and effective practice is to use these crystals as fillers (Shen et al., 2010). Yet why MOFs crystals cannot be directly used as fillers? The concern can be described in the following aspects. Firstly, the size of MOFs crystals is much smaller than the mesh of interwoven cellulose fibers, which might cause poor retention. Secondly, the properties of paper (uniformity, strength, durability) might be affected by the nanocrystals. Thirdly, the functionality of MOFs might be damaged after the paper-making procedures.
Herein, we propose the following strategy: MOFs were in situ formed in chitosan (CS) hydrogel, and the composite gel was added in the pulp and then transformed into MOFs-paper material. This strategy is feasible because the synthesis of some MOFs is compatible with the gelation process of CS (Zhang, Cai, Zhang, Ke, & Zhang, 2018). The hypothesis is that CS, a carbohydrate polymer with natural compatibility and affinity with cellulose pulp fibers (Shen et al., 2010), can protect MOFs crystals from being destroyed or washed away, and protect the matrix from the negative influence of crystal component. In the present work, one kind of MOFs known as ZIF-8 (zeolitic imidazolate framework-8) was selected as the model due to its well-known performance in adsorption, \(\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}\) capture and photocatalytic disinfection (Hao, Wu, Wang, & Liu, 2019). The strategy was evaluated in aspects of MOFs retention, paper properties and potential in air pollution control.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Zinc nitrate hexahydrate \(\mathrm{(Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O)}\) was purchased from Damao Chemical Co., Ltd. (Tianjing, China); 2-methylimidazole (2-HMeIM, \(\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}N_{2}})\) was purchased from Aladdin Industrial Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). All the chemical reagents mentioned above were of analytical grade and were used without further purification. NaOH and acetic acid were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. The softwood Kraft pulp used to make composite paper sheet was purchased from Shan Dong Pulp & Paper Co., Ltd. (Shandong, China), and the pulp was refined in a PFI refiner to the beating degree of \(35\,^{\circ}\mathrm{SR}\) . The average fiber length was \(2.133\ \mathrm{mm}\) , and the average fiber width was \(31.0~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) . Chitosan (CS) was purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. The average viscosity molecular weight \((M_{\eta})\) of CS is 1.19 \(\times\ 10^{6}\) Da, and degree of deacetylation (DD) is \(88~\%\) . Cationic retention aid (CPAM) Percol 182 was provided by Ciba (Ontario, Canada). The average \(M_{w}\) of CPAM was \(8.0\times10^{6}\) , and the substitution of CPAM was \(25\,\%\) .
