Method B, the retention rate was increased to \(18.08\,\%\), but was still unsatisfactory. The retention rate of Method C was estimated to be \(6.52\,\%\), indicating in situ deposition of ZIF-8 was not effective on the paper, probably because the fibers are interviewed and closely packed. The
retention rate of Method D was determined to be \(75.73\%\). The high retention rate can be attributed to the compatibility and affinity between CS and cellulose fibers (Shem et al., 2010), which protected MOFs crystals from being destroyed or washed away. These results confirmed this strategy is very favorable for the effective utilization of ZIF-8.
## 3.3. Paper properties
In addition to MOFs retention rate, paper materials are characterized by a wide range of properties. Some properties are crucial for air pollution control, including the affinity between ZIF-8 and the matrix, uniformity of composite paper and the mechanical strength. In this section, these properties were evaluated respectively.
Firstly, the affinity between ZIF-8 and the matrix was evaluated by bending, rubbing and flowing tests (Fig. 3a). The loss of cellulose and CS from paper is negligible, and the affinity between ZIF-8 and the matrix was presented by the weight loss. Composite paper prepared by composite gel showed the smallest weight loss. These results indicated that ZIF-8 particles had strong affinity to the matrix in Paper D.
Secondly, the uniformity of composite paper was evaluated and presented by evenness index (Fig. 3b). Evenness index is most often used to quantify formation quality, and higher evenness index indicated that the paper material had higher uniformity. The evenness index of paper generally decreases with the addition of fillers (Song et al., 2018). The composite paper prepared with composite gel showed the highest evenness index (Paper D in Fig. 3b). Paper D showed good uniformity (Fig. 2e) while flocculation was observed in Paper A and B (Fig. 2f, g) and an uneven layer was observed in Paper C (Fig. 2h).
Thirdly, the mechanical strength of composite paper was evaluated and present by tensile index. The mechanical strength of paper generally deteriorates with the addition of fillers, because fillers impact the bonding of cellulosic fibers (Song et al., 2018). It is noteworthy, however, the tensile index of composite paper prepared by composite gel was close to the value of pure cellulose paper (Fig. 3c), which was higher than other composite paper materials.
Combining the above results, the composite paper via composite gel is effective for utilization of ZIF-8, and has good filler-matrix affinity, uniformity and mechanical strength. These desirable properties are attributed to the structure of the composite paper (Fig. 4). CS can stop the MOFs from agglomeration and enhance the compatibility between MOFs and cellulose fibers.
## 3.4. Adsorption performance of composite paper
Adsorption is an important part of air pollution control. ZIF-8 crystals have large amounts of adsorption sites including organic adsorption sites and open metal sites which play a significant role to capture and adsorb gas molecules (Barea, Montoro, & Navarro, 2014). To verify whether MOFs can function properly in the composite gel and the composite paper, the absorption performance was evaluated. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) adsorption results were shown in Fig. 5 and Table 1. The composite gel and the composite paper all exhibited type I adsorption isotherms which was the same ZIF-8 crystals (Ma, Zhang et al., 2019). The ZIF-8 crystals had a high BET specific surface area of \(1091\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). The composite gel and composite paper reasonably showed lower value, since the they comprised both ZIF-8 and the carbohydrate components. The BET specific surface area of composite paper reached \(138.86\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) with only \(11.67\;\mathrm{wt.}\%\) of ZIF-8. Since the adsorption of the matrix is negligible, the adsorption ability was attributed to the functional fillers, indicating that MOFs were functioning properly in the composite material.
## 3.5. Filtration performance of composite paper
The filtration performance against PM was evaluated. The above sections were based on paper with basis weight of \(60\;\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-2}\), so this sample was used for initial evaluation. The filtration efficiency against PM with different aerodynamic diameters was shown in Fig. 6a. As a fibrous material, the matrix showed capture efficiency for \(\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}\) due to the physical barrier and adhesion effect (Batterman, Godwin, & Jia, 2005). Compared to the matrix, the removal efficiency of composite paper showed great improvement, because MOFs played a significant role with the accessible porosity and the large specific surface area (Song et al., 2018). More importantly, the improvement was even more evident for PM with smaller aerodynamic diameters. This is meaningful since PM with smaller diameters have a higher deposition efficiency in human body and could cause severe damages to neuronal cells (Jeon & Lee, 2016).
In addition to filtration efficiency, the air permeability was also evaluated (Fig. 6b) since it is closely related to performance of air filters. The air permeability slightly decreased with the addition of CS, since a small portion of the network might be blocked. However, the paper with composite gel showed much higher permeability (Paper D), indicating the porous crystals led to better air permeability. Without the help of CS (as demonstrated by Paper A), the permeability was actually lower due
to the poor retention of MOFs. Over all, the addition of composite gel is favorable in terms of air permeability.
The permeability and pressure drop can be modulated by changing the basis weight of composite paper. Fig. 6c demonstrated filtration
efficiency of composite paper with different basis weight. The porosity of composite paper increased with the decrease of basis weight, while the thickness of composite paper decreased (Table S4). When the basis weight was reduced \(50\,\%\), the filtration efficiency of \(\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}\) was \(98.8\,\%\); when the basis weight was reduced \(75\,\%\), the filtration efficiency of \(\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}\) was still \(86.1\,\%\), with air permeability increased \(300\,\%\) and pressure drop decreased \(93.1\,\%\) (to \(68.5\ \mathrm{Pa}\)). This indicated that the filtration efficiency and pressure drop can be conveniently modulated to reach a desirable filtration performance.
## 3.6. Photocatalytic disinfection performance of composite paper
The coexistence of pathogens with other pollutants (PM and VOCs, etc.) in natural air environment makes biological decontamination complicated and challenging (Mcdonald, Gouw, Gilman, Jathar, & Trainer, 2018). Limited inhibition ability against harmful microorganisms is a significant disadvantage for commonly used fibrous filters (Li et al., 2019). The filters become breeding ground for pathogenicity microorganism, with accumulated organic pollutants as nutrients, which causes second airborne contamination (Wang & Otani, 2013). Therefore, it is highly desired to develop filtration materials with good disinfection performance. The antibacterial activities of the composite paper were evaluated.
As shown in Fig. 7, clear inhibition zones were observed in the Petri dishes with ZIF-8 crystals. The antibacterial mechanism of ZIF-8 crystals could be attributed to the release of metal ions and organic ligands, resulting in the damage of membranes and the fragmentation of DNA (Ma et al., 2018). E. coli grew well on the surface of the Petri dish with cellulose paper, suggesting that under such condition it had no antibacterial activity against E. coli. However, clear inhibition zones in the Petri dishes with composite paper were observed. These results indicated that the composite paper has antibacterial ability and the ZIF-8 crystals in the composite paper were functioning well.
Additionally, it has been reported that ZIF-8 shows strong photocatalytic inactivation effect. The charge-trapping centers \(\mathrm{Zn}^{+}\)can be
photogenerated on MOF surface and effectively active \(O_{2}\) to form \(\cdot O_{2}^{-}\) and related reactive oxygen species (ROS) like \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). The ROS production contributes largely to the biocidal performance of ZIF-8 (Li et al., 2019). Encouraged by the excellent photocatalytic characteristic of ZIF-8, the photocatalytic antibacterial activity of the composite paper was also examined. Comparing the experiments conducted under light and dark conditions, the inhibition zone of the light group was larger than the dark group. This indicated that the antibacterial activity of ZIF-8 was obviously superior with light illumination. These results all together indicated that the composite paper has well inherited the photocatalytic characteristic of ZIF-8.
## 3.7. Other features and discussion
In addition to the performance evaluated above, this strategy also showed some desirable features which were discussed in this section.
Time and energy efficiency. Since the synthesis of MOFs was coupled with the gelation of CS, no extra preparation time was required. Subsequently, since the as prepared composite gel was added directly in the pulp, there is no need for extra treatment such as freeze drying. The desirable yield and retention of ZIF-8 avoided the waste of MOFs as well as the reactants to synthesize MOFs. These features make the method efficient in terms of time and energy.
Adaptability and Versatility. The coupling of ZIF-8 synthesis and CS gelation is not a unique case which cannot be adapted. Other MOFs/ CS composites can be obtained using different metal ions or ligand molecules, such as ZIF-7 and ZIF-67 (Zhang et al., 2018). It is an ongoing exploration to couple the synthesis of various MOFs with CS gelation. Moreover, composite gel with different kind of MOFs can be integrated into one paper. Although it is still challenging to process various MOFs with different functionalities into one device in an efficient way (Ma, Chai et al., 2019), this method provided a facile option to prepare multi-functional composite material.
Compatibility with existing paper-making industry. Since the composite gel was added directly in the pulp, this method requires no change in the existing paper-making procedures, and the composite paper can be manufactured in a continuous manner. These features endowed the method with compatibility with the paper-making industry and possibility of commercial scale production.
Environmental friendliness. The ZIF-8/CS composite gel was prepared by a DMF-free hydrothermal method. Considering that CS and cellulose are both nontoxic and biodegradable carbohydrate polymers, this method is favorable to sustainable development.
this method is favorable to sustainable development.
The comparison with other similar peers' work was also discussed in this section (Table 2). Representative peers' works were selected and analyzed. These works can be classified into two groups and be discussed respectively.
(1) The first class corresponded to cellulose-based 3D filtration materials (Ma, Zhang, Nie, Tan, Song et al., 2019, 2018; Su et al., 2018). These materials showed good filtration efficiency, and some showed relatively low pressure drop (Ma et al., 2018). However, some improvements had been attempted in the present work. Preparation Techniques. The preparation of these 3D filtration materials relied on freeze drying techniques. The present work is more favorable, considering energy consumption and manufacturing efficiency. The affinity between MOFs and the matrix. In these 3D filtration materials, the affinity between MOFs and the matrix still need improvement. In the present work, the affinity between MOFs and the matrix proved to be strong, which could avoid the loss of MOFs during processing and filtration. (2) The second class corresponded to filtration materials based synthetic polymers (Lee, Ojha, Oh, Kim, & Kim, 2020; Li et al., 2019; Xie et al., 2019; Zhang et al., 2016). Some of these materials had comparable filtration performance to our present work, and some showed outstanding filtration performance (Li et al., 2019). Nevertheless, there is obvious distinction between these materials and the present work. The filtration materials of this class were based on synthetic polymers, while the present work used cellulose as the matrix. We intended to achieve desirable filtration performance, and also to take advantage of the biodegradability and renewability of the natural polymers. Additionally, the present work can be considered as an additional choice to the tool box of the current preparation techniques.
## 4. Conclusion
In the present work, we proposed a strategy to in situ synthesize MOFs crystals in CS gel and to further prepare composite paper, striving to develop efficient and integrated air filters based on MOFs. This strategy is favorable for the effective utilization of ZIF-8, without hampering the intrinsic properties of paper. Moreover, the MOFs in the
composite paper material are functioning well in aspects of adsorption, filtration and sterilization. The combination of MOFs and cellulose paper endows the composite material with functionality, processability and flexibility, taking advantage of the potential of MOFs and the low-cost, biodegradable, renewable and highly industrialized matrix. This strategy may offer new ideas and reference for the design and preparation of MOFs/cellulose-based composite materials, which can provide protection against air hazards and ultimately benefit human health.
